    "question": {
      ":question/id": "b87edd95-c673-5c0c-85c7-e6f821ba240a",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+7 & =3x+7 \\\\ 3x+7-3x & =3x+7-3x \\\\ 7 & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $3x+7=3x+7$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7x-2 & =-7x-2 \\\\ -7x-2+7x & =-7x-2+7x \\\\ -2 & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-7x-2=-7x-2$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x-3 & =-3x-3 \\\\ -3x-3+3x & =-3x-3+3x \\\\ -3 & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-3x-3=-3x-3$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $3x+7=3x+7$; $-7x-2=-7x-2$; $-3x-3=-3x-3$ only.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
